good afternoon everyone my name is  Kirsty Sinclair Doutzen I'm a graduate  student here at Yale in the history of  our film and Media Studies program and  before I introduce the film I really did  want to say thank you so much to  everyone who's been involved in this  conference particularly Sofie Tatyana  Eric Tim for putting this together but  also all of the other speakers and  particularly thank you to David for your  wonderful keynote which is quite a hard  act to follow I have to say and as I've  said to a few of you over this weekend  I'm a strong believer that the first  time you see a film you should come  through it without any preconceptions  and that no prior critical  interpretation should affect your  initial responses to it a position  that's somewhat compromised by having to  introduce the film to you I thought it  would be interesting just to have a show  of hands how many of you have seen the  film before okay not many so for those  of you who haven't don't worry I'm not  gonna spoil anything hopefully but what  I thought I'd do instead of maybe  speaking too much about the production  history of the film or the plot which is  something we can do in the roundtable  afterwards I'm gonna try and reconstruct  how British audiences would have first  encountered this film in the autumn of  1944 and in the roundtable afters were  definitely going to talk a little bit  about how American audiences would have  encountered this when it was  subsequently released there in 1949 in a  slightly different version and I really  hoping we're gonna play a clip from that  murderd version because I think you'll  get a real kick out of it  I think it's far too easy now and we  watch films digitally projected on DVD  in a nicely lit lecture hall in  competition with lots of other screens  in the room to try and reconstruct what  it would have been like to see this film  originally so I'm just gonna ask you all  for a minute to imagine along with me  that we're going to the cinema in the  autumn of 1944 and this probably means  that we're  mostly young working-class northern  women if you can imagine it the people  that predominantly made up cinema  audiences in Britain at this time  perhaps you've been relocated away from  home in order to contribute to the war  effort and you've decided to visit one  of nearly 5,000 cinemas in operation in  Britain at this time perhaps you ride  the bus into town where a female  conductor takes your ticket just as the  woman staffing the box office will when  you arrive at the cinema as film  exhibition was almost entirely run by  women during World War two  as you enter the theater you pass from  one dark space into another from the  blacked-out City into the black box of  the cinema where both exist as spaces in  which all kinds of things can happen  away from the watchful eyes of parents  police and partners and of course the  theaters crowded as film audiences  swelled to unprecedented levels during  the war with high levels of unemployment  and little else to spend one's income on  so you find a seat at the back of the  theater near the projection booth a  dangerous place to sit in the days of  nitrate film stock and for those of you  that don't know nitrate is so flammable  it not only Auto ignites but once it's  ablaze it's actually almost impossible  to extinguish even when fully submerged  in water so beware so maybe you've  missed the newsreel and the B picture  and you've arrived almost halfway  through a Canterbury tale as we're still  well over a decade away from appointment  to view cinema where audiences would  arrive at the start of the film and  leave at the end but then perhaps you've  seen this film before wartime shortages  meant that Britain produced fewer than  50 films a year at this time while  audience numbers tripled so you look out  across a row of bobbing heads the  projector beam illuminated by the rising  smoke from carefully rationed cigarettes  and what do you see on the screen well  you see an image of  has a projector beam illuminated by  rising smoke from carefully rationed  cigarettes while this sequence which  occurs about an hour into your  Canterbury tale depicts not a movie  theater but a lantern slide lecture it's  hard not to think of this as a scene  that reflects the cinematic audience  back at themselves and while British  cinema during World War two is often  praised for its realism as documentary  filmmakers started working in commercial  studios  it seems that rather than films becoming  more like life life was increasingly  becoming more like cinema going so this  world smoke and charas Goro and  ascribing may in fact sound more like  the most iconic of American wartime  genres the film noir than a true Syrian  homefront film by Powell and Pressburger  but I think it's important to consider  the many noir elements that run  throughout the film not least as a way  of noting the craftsmanship of the  German production designer alfred  younger and cinematographer oh and  hillier but also as a way of thinking  about whether the British and American  film industries were truly in the same  boat at this time certainly both  Hollywood and the British film industry  shared some of the same limitations  during the war lack of person now as  technical staff and stars were called up  for service a lack of resources  particularly in Britain as a lot of the  studios were commandeered by the  military for storage space or as shadow  factories but also increased censorship  as both governments realize the power of  cinema to propagandize and also subvert  however one key difference which  separated Hollywood from Britain and one  that I think's best illuminated by  considering the noir elements of a  Canterbury tale is their anticipation of  the post-war landscape of their  respective industries of course  hollywood's turns of film noir was in  part due to the influx of German  immigrants relocating there during the  war but perhaps was more to do with the  paranoia felt in the Indus  three over the pending antitrust case  against the studios which had been  temporarily put on hold during the  conflict the Hollywood studio system was  well aware at the close of the war as  the close of the war approached that  it's days were numbered as governmental  clamp downs on their cartel practices  mostly block booking and blind selling  film so the exhibitors actually had very  little say over what they screened meant  that the heyday would soon be over  the British Studios on the other hand  had really never had it so good  far from trying to hamper the British  film industry the government had for  some time been trying to kick-start it  in order to rival the market dominance  of Hollywood and the Second World War  was the moment when they finally  succeeded as it became the first and  possibly only time when British motion  pictures outperformed American movies at  the box office and this is particularly  surprising given the fact that Hollywood  was more aggressively exporting its  films to Britain than before the war  eager to get a foothold in a market  where domestic production was  anticipated to be on the verge of  collapse now I think British cinema  during World War Two is often written  about as though the only filmmakers  working in Britain with the  documentarians at the crowne film unit  but I just want to remind you that there  were three commercial studios Ealing  Gainsborough and Alexander quarters  London films still operational during  the war and when we think about British  cinema in this period we can't just  discuss let me listen to Britain  Humphrey Jennings work fires were  started but we should also be thinking  about Gainsborough melodramas like the  man in grey and technical of spectacles  like the Thief of Bagdad and Henry v  which are all enormous hits at the box  office during this period so while we  might find in a Canterbury tale a  melancholy reminiscent of American film  noir perhaps it's due to the buoyancy of  the British film industry that paranoia  and nihilism have here been replaced  with optimism and cynicism has been  replaced with a mystic belief in  miracles and I don't think there's a lot  more I can say without ruining your  one tainted experience of the film but  I'll just leave you with a quote from  Pressburger when he introduced the film  himself at MoMA in 1980 where he  described his films as a nest in which  magic can settle I really look forward  to discussing the film with you all  after the screening
 

